  "worked_solution": "Let $D$ be the event that a selected cow is a dairy cow, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected cow is a dairy cow, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since $34%$ of all the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ are on ranch $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.34,\\,P(B)=0.36,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.25,\\,P(D|B)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is  a dairy cow is $37%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.37.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.37 & =(0.25)(0.34)+(0.2)(0.36)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.37 & =0.085+0.072+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.213 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow is $71%.$",
